{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 3, :comments ["<b>whose eye is opened (שְׁתֻם הָעָיִן)</b> – They found no source for this word in Scripture. Onkelos (Onkelos on Numbers 24:3) said \"דְּשַׁפִּיר חָזֵי\" (who sees clearly), like one whose eye is open. The language of the Sages (Avodah Zarah 69a): \"כְּדֵי שֶׁיִּשְׁתּוֹם וְיִסְתּוֹם\" (so that it may be opened and closed), meaning that the opening is widened. And perhaps it is a compound word from \"שִׁתִי אוֹתוֹתַי אֵלֶּה\" (Exodus 10:1) — שְׁתוּם meaning: placed, set — namely, the man who apprehends everything upon which he sets his eye."], :book :numbers, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 24, :notes {0 "The suggested etymology links שְׁתֻם to the root שית ('to set/place') in the Exodus verse; the final clause reads hyper-literally as 'שְׁתוּם מַה - הַגֶּבֶר שֶׁיַּשִּׂיג לְכָל מַה שֶׁיָּשִׁית עֵינוֹ,' where the wordplay on שית is untranslatable without paraphrase."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T04:08:21.617763071Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}